<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Svetozar Pribićević</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1875–1936</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Svetozar Pribicevic</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Svetozar Pribićević (1875–1936) was a Serb politician from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> who led the Croat-Serb Coalition in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a>. In 1918 he was vice-president of the National Council of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-of-slovenes-croats-and-serbs">State of Slovenes, Croats and Serbs</a> and pressed for its quick union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>. He served as a minister in the new kingdom, later broke with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>'s centralism and allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stjepan-radic">Stjepan Radić</a>, and died in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/svetozar-pribicevic.jpg" alt="Svetozar Pribićević" width="320" height="519" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Svetozar_Pribi%C4%87evi%C4%87_(1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
